== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Copper is the chemical element with symbol Cu (from Latin #emph[cuprum])
and atomic number 29. Among the dhatus, copper has distinctive
practical-chemical properties:

- #strong[Reddish-orange color]: distinctive among metals; the basis for
  its identification across all cultures
- #strong[High electrical conductivity]: second only to silver among
  metals; the basis of modern electrical wiring
- #strong[High thermal conductivity]: makes copper ideal for cooking
  vessels and heat exchangers
- #strong[Antimicrobial]: copper surfaces actively kill bacteria,
  viruses (including SARS-CoV-2), and fungi through the #emph[contact
  killing] mechanism (Cu²⁺ ions disrupt microbial membranes)
- #strong[Reactive but slow to corrode]: forms protective oxide and
  carbonate patinas (verdigris); the green patina on aged copper is
  Cu₂(CO₃)(OH)₂
- #strong[Easily worked]: malleable, ductile; ideal for casting
  (lost-wax bronze tradition) and hammering

Physical properties: - Density: 8.96 g/cm³ at 20°C - Melting point:
1085°C - Color: distinctive reddish-orange metallic - Hardness: 3.0 Mohs
(softer than iron but harder than gold/silver) - FCC (face-centered
cubic) crystal structure - Forms continuous solid solutions with: gold,
silver, nickel; partial solubility with: tin, zinc, aluminum

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], copper’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Tamra] (ताम्र) — most common technical term; cognate with
  English "tan" (color reference)
- #strong[Shulba] (शुल्ब) / #strong[Shulva] (शुल्व) — older Vedic term;
  refers to copper as construction metal
- #strong[Mleccha-mukha] (म्लेच्छ-मुख) — "foreign-faced," referring to
  imported high-quality copper
- #strong[Loha-vara] (लोह-वर) — "best of metals"; copper’s high status
  in practical metallurgy
- #strong[Udumbara] (उदुम्बर) — referring to copper’s color similar to
  udumbara fig
- #strong[Audumbara] (औदुम्बर) — cognate of above
- #strong[Ravipriya] (रविप्रिय) — "favored of the Sun" (alternative
  correspondence in some texts)
- #strong[Tamarakshatra] (ताम्ररक्षत्र) — copper-armor reference

The terminological emphasis on color and metallurgical excellence
reflects copper’s central role in classical practical work.

=== 1.3 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Copper corresponds to #strong[Mangala] (Mars) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. This correspondence is unanimous across primary
sources: - #emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3 -
#emph[Brihat Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy -
#emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] graha-rasa identifications - #emph[Charaka
Samhita] on therapeutic correspondences

#strong[The cross-tradition note] (continuing from Volume IV):

Western Hermetic alchemy: - Copper → Venus (love, beauty, soft, female)
- Iron → Mars (war, blood, hard, male)

Classical Indian jyotisha: - Copper → Mangala (war, blood, hard, male,
fire) — #emph[what the West called iron] - Silver → Shukra (refinement,
beauty) in some traditions - Iron → Shani (structure, time, density)

Both conventions have internal coherence, but they classify by different
criteria. The Indian convention tracks: - Color (copper-red ↔ Mars-red,
blood-red) - Physical reactivity (copper’s catalytic activity ↔
Mars-quality transformation) - Electrical/thermal conductivity (copper’s
high conductivity ↔ Mars-quality energy/fire) - Cultural use of copper
for ritual fire vessels (Mars-fire connection)

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification: - #strong[Mangala is
dhatu-graha] — the "structural/embodying" class (same as Chandra-silver,
Shani-iron)

Friendship pattern of Mangala in BPHS naisargika: - Mangala is friend
of: Surya (Sun), Chandra (Moon), Guru (Jupiter) - Mangala is enemy of:
Budha (Mercury) - Mangala is neutral to: Shukra (Venus), Shani (Saturn),
Rahu, Ketu

The #emph[enemy of Budha] status is structurally significant.
Copper-mercury operations are predicted to have specific timing-window
challenges. This shows up in the framework analysis of multi-component
preparations involving both copper and mercury (such as
#emph[swarna-makshika-bhasma] covered in §3.4 below).

=== 1.4 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Classical Indian copper sources were extensive: - #strong[Khetri belt
(Rajasthan)] — major copper mining region from antiquity through modern
era - #strong[Singhbhum (Jharkhand)] — copper sulfide deposits, mined
from very early period - #strong[Malanjkhand (Madhya Pradesh)] — modern
primary deposit - #strong[South Indian deposits] — smaller but
historically important

Indian copper metallurgy was technologically sophisticated: - Smelting
and casting traditions documented from Indus Valley period (~2500 BCE) -
Lost-wax casting (#emph[madhuchista vidhana]) of copper-bronze murtis as
one of the world’s most refined sculptural traditions - High-tin bronze
(Aranmula kannadi, ~32% tin) representing extreme precision in alloying
- Bell-bronze (beta-phase, 22-24% tin) for temple bells with specific
acoustic properties

