== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya], copper purification follows the standard
quenching sequence:

#strong[Procedure:] 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — heat copper foil to
red-hot, quench 7 times 2. #strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — quench 7 times
3. #strong[Go-mutra (cow’s urine)] — quench 7 times 4. #strong[Aranala
(fermented kanji)] — quench 7 times 5. #strong[Kulattha-kvatha
(horse-gram decoction)] — quench 7 times

Total: 35 quenching cycles.

For copper specifically, an additional vishesha shodhana step uses
#strong[Nimbu-swarasa (lemon juice)] for surface cleaning — citric acid
removes any green verdigris (basic copper carbonate) that may have
formed.

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

Copper’s higher reactivity than gold/silver means each quenching step
does more chemical work: - #strong[Sesame oil]: organic medium provides
reducing atmosphere; prevents oxidation during cooling -
#strong[Buttermilk]: lactic acid removes any oxide and sulfide surface
layers - #strong[Cow urine]: alkaline ammoniacal environment; copper
forms soluble complexes with ammonia (the basis of Schweizer’s reagent
in modern chemistry) - #strong[Kanji]: acidic, removes residual surface
contamination - #strong[Kulattha]: amino-acid mediated surface
conditioning

The cycling between oxidizing and reducing media is particularly
effective for copper because copper readily transitions between
oxidation states (Cu⁰, Cu⁺, Cu²⁺). The cycling actively works the copper
surface through repeated oxidation-reduction, removing impurities and
producing a microstructurally refined product.

#strong[XRD studies of shodhana intermediates] (Patil et al.) show that
conversion of copper metal to oxide phases begins during shodhana itself
— measurable Cu₂O and CuO peaks appear in XRD patterns of
partially-shodhana’d copper, even before formal marana begins.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standard procedure across licensed
Ayurvedic pharmacies.

=== 2.2 Marana (Incineration)
<marana-incineration>
The transformation from purified copper to #emph[tamra-bhasma] requires
extensive puta cycles. Documented procedures vary slightly but typically
require #strong[16-22 putas] to reach complete conversion.

#strong[Procedure (per Patil et al. 2024 and standardized references):]
1. #emph[Shuddha Tamra] (purified copper) is rolled into thin foils 2.
Triturated with mercury and sulfur (to form a kajjali-mediated paste) OR
with herbal juices alone (mercury-free protocols) 3. Cakes formed and
dried 4. Sealed in #emph[sharava-samputa] 5. Subjected to #emph[puta]
heating cycles 6. After each puta, characterization (traditional or
modern) determines whether further puta cycles are needed 7.
#strong[Critical sequence]: - Cycles 1-8: copper progressively oxidizes,
mostly to Cu₂O (cuprous oxide) - Cycles 9-16: continued oxidation, mixed
Cu₂O + CuO phases - Cycles 17-22: predominantly CuO (cupric oxide);
final phase

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

Tamra-bhasma synthesis is #emph[thermal oxidation of copper to copper
oxides] through repeated heating-cooling cycles. The phase transitions
are well-characterized:

#strong[Stage 1]: Cu (metallic) + O₂ → Cu₂O (cuprous oxide, brown-red
color) - Thermodynamically favored at moderate temperatures (200-700°C)
- Cu₂O is brick-red colored

#strong[Stage 2]: Cu₂O + ½O₂ → 2CuO (cupric oxide, black) - Favored at
higher temperatures (\>700°C) and prolonged exposure - CuO is black
colored

The classical procedure achieves complete conversion by cycling through
these stages multiple times. Each puta cycle: - Heating exposes interior
copper (or partially-oxidized copper) to oxygen - Cooling allows phase
stabilization - Trituration breaks up agglomerates and exposes fresh
surface - Repeated cycles produce nano-scale particles

#strong[Modern characterization (peer-reviewed):]

#align(center)[#table(
  columns: 3,
  align: (col, row) => (auto,auto,auto,).at(col),
  inset: 6pt,
  [Reference], [Method], [Findings],
  [Singh et al. 2010 (Pharmacog Rev)],
  [XRD, SEM, EDX, XPS, IR, TG],
  [Predominantly CuO; some Cu₂O residual; particles in 100-500 nm range
  with nano features],
  [Patil et al. 2024 (IJPSR)],
  [XRD across puta cycles],
  [Cu₂O dominant at puta 16, transitions to CuO by puta 22; complete
  sequence documented],
  [Open Access Pub 2025],
  [XRD characterization],
  [HgS and CuS as major crystalline phases when mercury-mediated route
  used; Cu₄O₃ as minor phase],
  [Multiple antimicrobial studies],
  [Various assays],
  [Antimicrobial activity confirmed; gastric ulcer protection,
  hepatoprotective effects],
)
]

#strong[Convergent finding]: traditional tamra-bhasma is
#strong[predominantly CuO (cupric oxide) at the nano-to-micro scale]
with some Cu₂O depending on processing completeness. The black color of
fully-processed tamra-bhasma reflects the CuO phase (Cu₂O being
red-brown, CuO being black). The traditional tests confirm phase
transformation; modern XRD characterization makes the phase
identification explicit.

#strong[Mercury-mediated route distinction]: when kajjali is used during
marana, the resulting bhasma contains HgS (mercury sulfide) and CuS
(copper sulfide) phases in addition to CuO. This is a chemically
distinct preparation from pure copper-oxide tamra-bhasma. The classical
texts allow both routes; modern practice varies.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Multiple modern studies confirm
consistent characterization across batches.

=== 2.3 The traditional confirmation tests
<the-traditional-confirmation-tests>
The standard tests apply: #emph[rekhapurnatva], #emph[varitaratva],
#emph[apunarbhava], #emph[niruttha], #emph[nirdhuma], #emph[niswadu],
#emph[nischandratva]. Plus copper-specific test: - #emph[Nirdhuma]
particularly important — tamra-bhasma must produce no smoke when heated,
indicating complete conversion (incompletely converted bhasma releases
sulfur or organic vapors)

These tests effectively distinguish complete-conversion tamra-bhasma
from incompletely-processed copper or simple copper oxide. Modern XRD
validates the same phase composition that the traditional tests
indirectly measure.

