== 3. Major Copper Preparations
<major-copper-preparations>
=== 3.1 Tamra-Bhasma (Copper Calx / Copper Oxide Nanoparticles)
<tamra-bhasma-copper-calx-copper-oxide-nanoparticles>
#strong[Composition:] Predominantly CuO (cupric oxide); some Cu₂O may
remain; mercury-mediated routes add HgS and CuS phases \
#strong[Color:] Black (pure CuO) to dark brown (mixed Cu₂O + CuO) \
#strong[Particle size:] 100-500 nm with nanoscale features \
#strong[Source text:] Rasaratna Samuccaya, Bhaishajya Ratnavali, Rasa
Tarangini

#strong[Therapeutic indications (classical):] - #emph[Pleehavriddhi]
(splenomegaly) — primary indication - #emph[Yakrit-roga] (liver
disorders) — primary indication - #emph[Kushtha] (skin diseases) -
#emph[Krimi] (parasitic infections) - #emph[Udara-roga] (abdominal
disorders, including gastric ulcers) - #emph[Shotha] (edema) -
#emph[Pandu] (anemia, especially when combined with iron)

#strong[Modern pharmacology research:] - #strong[Anti-ulcer activity]:
well-documented; tamra-bhasma reduces gastric acid secretion and
protects gastric mucosa - #strong[Hepatoprotective effects]: confirmed
in CCl₄-induced rat liver injury models (Patil et al.) -
#strong[Free-radical scavenging activity]: documented through standard
assays - #strong[Antimicrobial]: copper compounds have established
antimicrobial activity; classical use of copper vessels for water
storage is bioactively grounded - #strong[Anti-cancer]: preliminary
studies show activity against various cancer cell lines - #strong[Lipid
peroxidation modulation]: documented in animal studies

#strong[Dosage:] 30-125 mg twice daily classical; modern licensed
Ayurvedic practice typically 60-125 mg daily under physician supervision

#strong[Toxicity:] Copper is essential for human health (recommended
daily intake ~0.9 mg adult), but excess copper is toxic. Concerns: -
Copper toxicity (Wilson’s disease, hepatic copper overload) - GI
irritation possible with high doses - Should not be used in patients
with Wilson’s disease or copper overload conditions - Long-term use
should monitor serum ceruloplasmin and copper levels

The toxicity profile is moderate — better than mercury but more
concerning than iron or noble metals.

=== 3.2 Tamra Sindura (Copper Vermillion)
<tamra-sindura-copper-vermillion>
#strong[Composition:] Mercury-mediated copper preparation containing
HgS, CuS, and copper-mercury-sulfur intermediate phases \
#strong[Use:] Specific therapeutic indications, especially for chronic
skin conditions and certain digestive disorders

#strong[Note:] This is different from regular tamra-bhasma in that
mercury is intentionally retained as part of the active preparation
rather than being driven off completely. Modern characterization shows
persistent HgS and CuS phases.

=== 3.3 Tamra-Vanga (Copper-Tin alloys)
<tamra-vanga-copper-tin-alloys>
Several classical preparations involve copper-tin alloys at specific
compositions: - #strong[Bronze] (general): 75-90% Cu, 10-25% Sn — used
for vessels, bells, statues - #strong[Bell-bronze (beta phase)]: 76-78%
Cu, 22-24% Sn — used for temple bells (see §4.2) - #strong[Mirror-bronze
(delta phase)]: 67-68% Cu, 32-33% Sn — used for Aranmula kannadi mirrors
(see §4.1)

Two-graha (Mangala + Guru in Indian convention; tin \= Guru).
Mangala-Guru is #emph[friend] both directions, making copper-tin alloys
among the more stable graha pairs. This is significant for both
bell-bronze acoustic properties and mirror-bronze specular reflectance.

=== 3.4 Swarna-Makshika-Bhasma (Chalcopyrite Calx)
<swarna-makshika-bhasma-chalcopyrite-calx>
Despite "swarna" (gold) in the name, #strong[swarnamakshika is
chalcopyrite] (CuFeS₂) — a copper-iron-sulfide mineral, not gold. The
name reflects the golden surface luster.

#strong[Composition after processing]: Fe₂O₃, FeS₂, CuS, SiO₂ (per Singh
et al. 2018) \
#strong[Use:] Classical indications include #emph[pandu] (anemia),
#emph[kushtha] (skin), #emph[netraroga] (eye diseases)

This preparation involves three-graha integration (copper-Mangala +
iron-Shani + sulfur via Mangala). Mangala-Shani is #emph[neutral];
Mangala-itself amplification is consistent. The framework predicts
moderate timing flexibility for swarnamakshika operations.

=== 3.5 Copper in Sacred Alloys
<copper-in-sacred-alloys>
Copper appears as a primary component in #strong[panchaloha] (typically
80% by mass, the largest single component) and in #strong[ashtadhatu]
(12.5% by mass, equal-weighted with 7 other metals).

#strong[Copper’s contribution]: - Dominant mass-fraction in panchaloha;
provides bulk structural metal - Mangala-graha contribution to the
multi-metal cosmological integration - Mangala’s enmity with Budha
(Mercury) creates specific tensions in mercury-copper preparations

#strong[The framework’s panchaloha analysis findings (50% mass-weighted,
92% equal-graha-weighted)]: The mass-weighted scoring biases predictions
toward Mars-friendly nakshatras (Mrigashira, Chitra, Dhanishtha) because
copper dominates by mass (80%). The equal-graha-weighting recovers the
tradition’s prescribed list at 92% — #emph[because the tradition treats
panchaloha as graha-integration regardless of mass fraction]. This is
one of the most important findings in the entire framework analysis:
copper’s mass dominance does NOT determine the cosmological priority;
classical practice integrates each component graha equally.

