== 4. Special Cases: Aranmula Kannadi and Bell-Bronze
<special-cases-aranmula-kannadi-and-bell-bronze>
=== 4.1 Aranmula Kannadi (Delta High-Tin Bronze Mirror)
<aranmula-kannadi-delta-high-tin-bronze-mirror>
The Aranmula kannadi is a specular metallic mirror made from
#strong[delta-phase high-tin bronze] (Cu₃₁Sn₈, 32.6% tin). Produced by a
single workshop family in the Pathanamthitta region of Kerala. Studied
extensively by Sharada Srinivasan at NIAS Bangalore since 1990.

This case is treated extensively in `extended_recovery_models.py` of the
Atlas framework. Cross-reference: see Volume on Recovery Models for full
technical analysis.

#strong[Key features:] - Exact 32.6% tin composition (intermetallic
delta phase Cu₃₁Sn₈) - Cubic crystal structure (space group F-43m) -
Specular reflectance ~80% in visible spectrum - Produces distortion-free
mirror images - Brittle — shatters if dropped, requires careful
polishing - Considered auspicious — traditionally one of
#emph[ashtamangalyam] (8 sacred items) of Kerala - Composition tightly
controlled — small deviation from 32.6% causes failure - Continuous use
1st millennium BC to present

#strong[Modern materials science understanding:] - Cu₃₁Sn₈ is a
stoichiometric intermetallic compound with ordered crystal structure -
High specular reflectance comes from this ordered structure plus
polishing to atomic-scale flatness - Composition window narrow (~32-33%
tin) - Cooling rate matters: too fast → retained beta phase; too slow →
peritectic decomposition - Quenching from ~600-700°C preserves delta
phase - Polishing must achieve \<100 nm surface roughness for true
specular reflection

#strong[The framework’s contribution]:

Aranmula kannadi is two-graha (Mangala + Guru). Mangala-Guru is
#emph[friend] both directions — one of the more harmonious graha pairs.

Framework predicts strong favorability across many nakshatras for
Aranmula casting: - #strong[Most favorable]: Uttara Phalguni (Surya —
friend of both Mangala and Guru), Uttara Ashadha (Surya), Mrigashira
(Mangala — own-graha), Punarvasu (Guru — own-graha) - #strong[Strong]:
Dhruva nakshatras generally (Rohini, Uttara Phalguni, Uttara Ashadha,
Uttara Bhadrapada) - #strong[Avoid]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati
(Budha-ruled, Budha enemy of Mangala)

#strong[Validation opportunity]: the Aranmula workshop family currently
operates. Their casting dates can be tracked. Framework predicts which
dates should produce best-quality outputs (clearest specularity, fewest
casting defects). These predictions can be validated against the
workshop’s own QC records over time. This is the cleanest
direct-validation case in the entire compendium.

#strong[Preservation value]: documenting the framework’s predictions for
this living tradition creates a knowledge-preservation backup. If the
family lineage breaks, framework + Srinivasan’s published documentation
provides a path to restart.

#strong[Natural collaboration target]: Sharada Srinivasan at NIAS
Bangalore.

=== 4.2 Bell-Bronze (Beta-Phase High-Tin Bronze)
<bell-bronze-beta-phase-high-tin-bronze>
#strong[Composition]: 22-24% tin in copper (beta-phase Cu-Sn solid
solution) \
#strong[Use]: Temple bells, ritual gongs, musical instruments throughout
South India \
#strong[Properties]: - Wrought-and-quenched form gives golden lustre and
improved tensile strength vs as-cast - Acoustically distinct — specific
harmonic structure highly valued - Used for Kerala temple bells, ritual
vessels - Documented in Srinivasan 1995 (J Historical Metallurgy 29(2))
and Srinivasan 2013 (Trans Indian Inst Metals)

#strong[Acoustic significance]: The high-tin beta-bronze produces
specific harmonic structure that distinguishes it from lower-tin
bronzes. The fundamental frequency, overtone series, and decay
characteristics are functions of: - Composition (Cu/Sn ratio) -
Microstructure (grain size, phase distribution) - Geometry (bell shape,
thickness, mouth diameter) - Cooling profile after casting (affects
retained phase)

#strong[Framework’s contribution]:

Bell-bronze is two-graha (Mangala + Guru), same as Aranmula but at lower
tin content. Same framework predictions apply.

#strong[This is the most empirically accessible test case in the entire
compendium]: bell acoustic properties are #emph[measurable with phone +
FFT analysis] (essentially zero equipment cost). Bells cast on
framework-favorable vs unfavorable dates could be compared by: -
Fundamental frequency - Overtone structure (presence/absence of specific
harmonics) - Decay characteristics (how quickly specific frequencies
fade) - Subjective tonal quality (assessed by musicians)

Framework predictions for bell-casting could be tested by acoustic
analysis without specialized equipment. #emph[Recommended near-term
validation target.]

=== 4.3 Copper Vessels for Water Storage
<copper-vessels-for-water-storage>
Classical Indian tradition specifies copper water vessels
(#emph[tamra-jal-patra]) for storage of drinking water. This practice
has documented antimicrobial basis: - Copper surfaces actively kill
#emph[E. coli], #emph[Salmonella], #emph[V. cholerae], #emph[S. aureus],
and other waterborne pathogens - Killing mechanism: Cu²⁺ ions disrupt
microbial membranes; ROS generation - Storage time matters: 6-8 hours
minimum for substantial bacterial reduction - Modern WHO research has
confirmed copper vessel water storage as effective point-of-use water
treatment

#strong[The classical practice anticipates modern public health
understanding by ~3000 years.] This is one of the cleanest convergence
cases between classical Indian tradition and modern science.

