== 5. Framework Timing Predictions
<framework-timing-predictions>
=== 5.1 Methodology
<methodology>
Applying the Atlas computational framework to copper operations: copper
is single-graha (Mangala) for the primary substance, with co-substance
contributions varying by procedure.

=== 5.2 Pure copper operations (shodhana, simple bhasma)
<pure-copper-operations-shodhana-simple-bhasma>
Single-graha analysis (Mangala-only). Friendship from BPHS: - Mangala is
friend of: Surya (Sun), Chandra (Moon), Guru (Jupiter) - Mangala is
enemy of: Budha (Mercury) - Mangala is neutral to: Shukra (Venus), Shani
(Saturn), Rahu, Ketu

Framework predicts: - #strong[Most favorable]: Krittika, Uttara
Phalguni, Uttara Ashadha (Surya-ruled, Surya-Mangala friend); Rohini,
Hasta, Shravana (Chandra-ruled, Chandra-Mangala friend); Punarvasu,
Vishakha, Purva Bhadrapada (Guru-ruled, Guru-Mangala friend);
Mrigashira, Chitra, Dhanishtha (Mangala-ruled, own-graha) -
#strong[Strong]: Bharani, Purva Phalguni, Purva Ashadha (Shukra-ruled,
neutral); Pushya, Anuradha, Uttara Bhadrapada (Shani-ruled, neutral) -
#strong[Avoid]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati (Budha-ruled — Budha enemy of
Mangala); Ardra, Swati, Shatabhisha (Rahu-ruled, neutral but with
potential complications)

Copper’s friendship pattern is favorable across many nakshatras, making
copper operations relatively timing-flexible compared to iron
operations. The single enmity is with Mercury (Budha), creating a small
"avoid" window but leaving most nakshatras workable.

=== 5.3 Copper-mercury operations (when mercury-mediated route is used)
<copper-mercury-operations-when-mercury-mediated-route-is-used>
Two-graha (Mangala + Budha). Mangala-Budha is #emph[enemy] both
directions. This creates tension: nakshatras good for the operation must
be friendly to BOTH simultaneously, which is rare.

Framework predicts: - #strong[Most favorable]: Pushya, Anuradha, Uttara
Bhadrapada (Shani-ruled — Shani is neutral to both; Dhruva
activity-class adds bonus) - #strong[Strong secondary]: Punarvasu,
Vishakha, Purva Bhadrapada (Guru-ruled — Guru friend of Mangala, neutral
to Budha) - #strong[Avoid]: Surya, Chandra-ruled nakshatras (these favor
Mangala but are problematic for Budha tension); Mangala-own nakshatras
(amplify the enmity)

This prediction matches the pattern from other multi-graha mercurial
operations: Pushya emerges as the optimal stable anchor when graha-pair
tensions are present. Volume I (kajjali), Volume II (makaradhwaja),
Volume III (rajata-bhasma three-graha), Volume IV (lauha-mercury
operations), and now Volume V (tamra-mercury operations) — #emph[Pushya
consistently emerges as the framework’s top recommendation for
multi-graha mercurial preparations]. This cross-volume consistency is
itself evidence of the framework’s internal coherence.

=== 5.4 Copper-mercury-sulfur operations (mercury-mediated tamra-bhasma)
<copper-mercury-sulfur-operations-mercury-mediated-tamra-bhasma>
Three-graha (Mangala + Budha + Mangala-via-sulfur). Sulfur is sometimes
assigned Mangala correspondence too (fire-element, transformative).
Effectively this is dominated by Mangala + Budha tension as in §5.3,
with reinforced Mangala influence.

Framework predicts same general pattern: Shani-ruled and Guru-ruled
nakshatras provide stable anchors; Budha-Mangala enmity is the dominant
tension to navigate.

=== 5.5 Copper-tin operations (Aranmula kannadi, bell-bronze)
<copper-tin-operations-aranmula-kannadi-bell-bronze>
Two-graha (Mangala + Guru). Mangala-Guru is #emph[friend] both
directions.

Framework predicts strong favorability across many nakshatras
(cross-reference §4.1): - #strong[Most favorable]: Uttara Phalguni,
Uttara Ashadha (Surya-ruled — friend to both); Punarvasu, Vishakha,
Purva Bhadrapada (Guru-ruled — own-graha for Guru); Mrigashira, Chitra,
Dhanishtha (Mangala-ruled — own-graha for Mangala) - #strong[Strong]:
Dhruva nakshatras (especially Uttara Phalguni and Uttara Ashadha);
Rohini, Hasta, Shravana (Chandra-ruled — friend of Mangala) -
#strong[Avoid]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati (Budha-ruled — Budha enemy of
Mangala)

This is the framework’s strongest prediction set for copper-based alloy
operations. Bell-bronze and Aranmula kannadi production should
preferentially occur on these nakshatras for optimal results.

=== 5.6 Copper in panchaloha/ashtadhatu
<copper-in-panchalohaashtadhatu>
Volume I §4.4 detailed the panchaloha analysis. Key finding: copper’s
80% mass-fraction biases mass-weighted scoring toward Mangala-friendly
nakshatras (50% precision); equal-graha-weighting (cosmological purpose)
gives 91.7% precision matching ashtadhatu pattern. Copper’s role in the
alloy is not its mass dominance but its Mangala-graha contribution
alongside seven other equal-weighted graha-contributions.

